The development team requires specific logs stored within the Nautilus storage server situated in the Stratos DC. Access the designated location on the server to retrieve the necessary logs. Further, perform below actions:


Create a tar archive named logs.tar (under natasha's home) of /var/log/ directory.
Now, create a compressed tar archive as well named logs.tar.gz (under natasha's home) of /var/log/ directory.
To complete this task on the Nautilus storage server, you need to create two archives in natasha's home directory from /var/log. Since /var/log contains files owned by root and some subdirectories may not be readable, run the commands with sudo.

📦 Step 1: Create the uncompressed tar archive
bash
sudo tar -cvf /home/natasha/logs.tar /var/log/
-c creates a new archive

-v shows progress (optional but helpful)

-f specifies the archive filename

🗜️ Step 2: Create the compressed tar archive
bash
sudo tar -czvf /home/natasha/logs.tar.gz /var/log/
-z compresses with gzip

The rest of the flags are the same as above

🔑 Step 3: Ensure natasha owns the archives
Since the archives were created with sudo, they will be owned by root. Fix ownership so natasha can access them:

bash
sudo chown natasha:natasha /home/natasha/logs.tar /home/natasha/logs.tar.gz
✅ Verification
List the files and check their sizes:

bash
ls -lh /home/natasha/logs.tar /home/natasha/logs.tar.gz
Expected output shows both archives owned by natasha, with logs.tar.gz noticeably smaller than logs.tar due to compression. You can also inspect the archive contents without extracting:

bash
tar -tf /home/natasha/logs.tar | head
tar -tzf /home/natasha/logs.tar.gz | head
💡 Notes
Both archives are placed directly under /home/natasha/ (natasha's home), as required.

If you prefer to create the archives as natasha without sudo, natasha would need read access to all of /var/log, which is typically not the case — so using sudo plus chown is the reliable approach.

There is some data on Nautilus App Server 3 in Stratos DC. Data needs to be altered in some of the files. On Nautilus App Server 3, alter the /home/BSD.txt file as per details given below.



a. Delete all lines containing the word copyright and save the results in /home/BSD_DELETE.txt file. (Please be aware of case sensitivity)

b. Replace all occurrences of the word or (look for the exact match) with is and save the results in /home/BSD_REPLACE.txt file.

Note: Let's say you are asked to replace the word to with from. In that case, make sure not to alter any words containing the string itself, for example; upto, contributor etc.
To alter the /home/BSD.txt file as required on Nautilus App Server 3, use grep and sed. The case-sensitivity and exact-word-match requirements are the key details here.

a. Delete all lines containing the word "copyright" (case-sensitive)
bash
grep -v 'copyright' /home/BSD.txt > /home/BSD_DELETE.txt
-v inverts the match, so only lines without the word copyright are kept.

No -i flag is used, so matching is case-sensitive — lines containing Copyright or COPYRIGHT will not be deleted, as per the requirement.

Verification:

bash
grep -c 'copyright' /home/BSD_DELETE.txt   # should return 0
b. Replace the exact word "or" with "is"
bash
sed 's/\bor\b/is/g' /home/BSD.txt > /home/BSD_REPLACE.txt
\b is a word boundary, ensuring only the standalone word or is matched.

g applies the replacement to all occurrences on each line.

This prevents altering words like upto, contributor, order, etc., because those don't have word boundaries around or.

Verification:

bash
grep -o '\bor\b' /home/BSD_REPLACE.txt | wc -l   # should return 0
To confirm that words containing "or" were left untouched:

bash
grep -o '\b[a-zA-Z]*or[a-zA-Z]*\b' /home/BSD_REPLACE.txt | sort -u | head
You should still see words like order, contributor, upto unchanged, while standalone or has become is.

⚠️ Notes
If sed on your system doesn't support \b (GNU sed does), use \< and \> instead:

bash
sed 's/\<or\>/is/g' /home/BSD.txt > /home/BSD_REPLACE.txt
Both output files are written directly under /home/, as specified, without modifying the original /home/BSD.txt.

If you don't have write permission to /home/, prefix the commands with sudo or run them as a user with appropriate access.
During the security audit last week, the security team found some issues on one of the app servers in Stratos DC. They shared a report and asked to apply some resource limits for some users.



Configure a resource limit (hard limit) for the developers group so that this group cannot exceed 5 logins (maxlogins) on App server 3 in Stratos Datacenter.
To enforce a hard limit on simultaneous logins for the developers group on App Server 3, you need to edit the PAM limits configuration file.

⚙️ Configuration Steps
Log in to App Server 3 and switch to the root user.

Edit the limits configuration file:

bash
sudo vi /etc/security/limits.conf
Add the following line at the end of the file:

text
@developers hard maxlogins 5
@developers: The @ symbol specifies that the domain is a group .

hard: Enforces the absolute maximum limit .

maxlogins: The item that controls the maximum number of simultaneous logins .

5: The maximum number of concurrent sessions allowed for the group.

Save and exit the file.

✅ Verification
The new limit will be enforced the next time a member of the developers group initiates a new login session. You can verify the configuration is present:

bash
grep developers /etc/security/limits.conf
Note: Group limits set in this file do not apply to the root user . If you need to test the limit, you must log in as a non-root user who is a member of the developers group.
xFusionCorp Industries is planning to host two static websites on their infra in Stratos Datacenter. 
The development of these websites is still in-progress, but we want to install some prerequisites to get the servers ready. Please perform the following steps to accomplish the task:

Install httpd package and dependencies on app server 3 and start its service.
To install and start the httpd web server on App Server 3, follow these steps.

📦 Step 1: Install httpd
bash
sudo yum install -y httpd
This installs the Apache HTTP Server (httpd) along with its dependencies.

▶️ Step 2: Start the httpd service
bash
sudo systemctl start httpd
🔁 Step 3: Enable the service at boot (recommended)
Since two websites will be hosted and the server will be rebooted during maintenance, enabling the service ensures it starts automatically:

bash
sudo systemctl enable httpd
✅ Step 4: Verify the service is running
bash
sudo systemctl status httpd
Expected output includes Active: active (running).

You can also verify the package is installed and the server responds:

bash
rpm -q httpd
curl -I http://localhost
rpm -q httpd should print the installed version, e.g. httpd-2.x.x-....

The curl command should return HTTP/1.1 200 OK (or a similar success response) from the default Apache page.

💡 Notes
If firewalld is running on the server, you may need to allow HTTP traffic for the websites to be reachable externally:

bash
sudo firewall-cmd --permanent --add-service=http
sudo firewall-cmd --reload
The default document root is /var/www/html, which is where the static website content can later be placed. The web server is now ready for the next steps of hosting the two static sites.

During recent servers audit, its was observed that some cleanup is needed on all app servers in Stratos Datacenter. Find below more details:



Remove logrotate package from all app servers in Stratos Datacenter.
To remove the logrotate package from all app servers in the Stratos Datacenter, you need to run the removal command on each app server individually.

🖥️ App Servers in Stratos DC
The app servers are typically:

stapp01 (App Server 1)

stapp02 (App Server 2)

stapp03 (App Server 3)

🗑️ Removal Command
On each app server, run:

bash
sudo yum remove -y logrotate
remove uninstalls the package (and anything dependent on it, if applicable).

-y automatically answers "yes" to the confirmation prompt.

✅ Verification
On each server, confirm the package is gone:

bash
rpm -q logrotate
Expected output:

text
package logrotate is not installed
💡 Notes
Be aware: logrotate is a dependency of some system packages. yum remove will list any packages that depend on it and ask to remove them too. Review that list before confirming — on standard RHEL/CentOS systems this is usually safe, but double-check nothing critical is being pulled out.

If you have SSH access and centralized management, you can loop over all servers from a jump host:

bash
for host in stapp01 stapp02 stapp03; do
    ssh $host "sudo yum remove -y logrotate"
done
(Adjust usernames/hostnames to match your environment.)

After removal, log rotation will no longer be handled automatically. Since this is an audit-driven cleanup, ensure the relevant teams are aware, and consider an alternative log rotation mechanism if logs still need managing.

